{
  "image_name": "0f3ed9c1cdcb319058ff30f43c3a4590.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how developing T cells move through the thymus and are guided by stromal and immune cells. The upper schematic shows thymocytes traveling from the cortex, through the cortico-medullary junction, into the medulla while changing developmental state. Early double-negative cells appear near the outer cortex and progress to double-positive cells, then to single-positive CD4+ or CD8+ cells. Their path is shaped by two key checkpoints: positive selection in the cortex and negative selection closer to the medulla. Cell proliferation is depicted as high in the cortex and very low in the medulla.\n\nThe surrounding thymic environment includes cortical and medullary epithelial cells, dendritic cells, and macrophages, suggesting that different niches support survival, selection, and final maturation. Curved arrows indicate migration between regions rather than a static arrangement.\n\nThe lower panel highlights the main kinds of signals exchanged between a thymocyte and a thymic epithelial cell. These include direct receptor-ligand binding such as TCR–MHC, Notch, and integrin-related adhesion, as well as cues from extracellular matrix proteins, chemokines, and soluble cytokines or hormones. Overall, the image emphasizes that T-cell development depends on coordinated migration, selective screening, and multiple cell-contact and secreted signals within distinct thymic compartments.",
